rty later  jodaro exudes all the emotion of a plank  of wood and  in my first experience with the anime  this element bothered me to a point of  despising him  however this time around with the manga  i felt that because of that stoicism the  brief moments where the cracks start to  show in his persona  actually make a big impression on the  reader with my personal favorite example  of this being observed in the  relationship he has with his own mother  a number of times during the first 10  chapters he openly and loudly dismisses  his mother like the stereotypical  delinquent he's portrayed to be  however when he notices that she looks  pale and when she doesn't show up to  kiss him  he wonders where she's gone to while  these examples are in the anime there  are others that aren't quite as overt  these are small moments in isolation but  they paint a bigger picture of a  character who is virtuous and kind  behind that masquerade and it is by far  my favorite aspect  of his character however for most people  their favorite aspect of the story is  not jotaro  while many might love him or think he's  awesome because okay he is i'd be  willing to wager that this arc is as  iconic as it is because of one specific  element  i've neglected to talk about thus far  stance  this is largely why this is my favorite  setup out of all the jojo arcs thus far  and  not only does this act as our  inauguration to the character of jodaro  but also facilitates introductions to  much of the cast establishes  relationships and impresses upon us one  very new addition to the series  the iconic jojo's bizarre adventure  stand  magic system stands as a system of power  and battle are much more easily  diversifiable than hammond was  and thanks to iraqis wonderful  implementation of them they have become  synonymous with jojo's bizarre adventure  at this point acting effectively as  manifestations of one's own personal  willpower they come in all different  shapes and sizes and everyone gets their  own unique stand  jodoro stan comes to be known as star  platinum and while he's the most simple  of all the stands we see in this series  in that he is super fast and punches  really hard his appearances are made to  look all the more impressive because his  intense  and fast movements stand in direct  contrast with jonaro's reserved and  stoic persona once again jodaro's  personality serves to enhance more  elements of this story  um  [Music]  and this story is full of stance hamon  is old news and stans are here to stay  once the goal of traveling to egypt to  do battle with dio and to stop his evil  ways comes to the forefront of our  heroes minds  pretty much every other character we  meet along the way has a stand  and you best believe they more often  than not are sent by dio to stop the  team of jotaro joseph kakyoin abdul and  panorah from reaching  egypt this is a story three generations  in the making dio now wielding the body  of joseph's grandfather  jonathan after he sacrificed himself for  the betterment of his family and mankind  is now the body puppet of dio who is in  the manga depicted in shadow from  multiple volumes before he's eventually  revealed properly it's a great premise  for a story and one i am totally  on board with now some of you out there  who might be new to this series might be  asking  okay stands are cool and all but what  about hammond why do i see a distinct  lack of hammond and  since zack morris can freeze time does  he have a stand too all perfectly valid  questions  and while i can speak with absolute  certainty regarding sac morris's  standing in the jojo continuity when it  comes to the disappearance of hammond i  think it's an understandable question  for fans of the series to ask  what gives where's my hammond because  joseph and jonathan spent  massive sections of their respective  stories learning these fundamental  elements of the system  in order to compete against the evil  that they came up against and  in stardust crusaders hamon is barely  ever mentioned i can see why this might  have robbed fans of ham on the wrong way  however now after reading up to this  point in the series and after reading  araki's book  i think that's with good reason you see  jojo's bizarre adventure is structured  somewhat  unconventionally compared with other  long-running shonen  in that its main character consistently  changes from story act to story arc  this does wonders for the series in  keeping it feeling distinct from other  popular series within the issue of  weekly shonen jump  but it regrettably has i believe an  irritating side effect  specifically when it came to  implementing the hammond power system  there are obvious benefits in switching  from hamon to stand  powers when araki as a writer adopted  stans they granted him the flexibility  to give any person any ability  which automatically injects tons of  opportunity for creative storytelling  however i don't believe this to be the  only positive that influenced that  decision to shift gears entirely  i mean let's not forget jojo switching  from hammond to stands would be like if  dragon ball switched from key to pokemon  battles  vegeta look a pokemon  i'm not a pokemon i'm shout soon so  why the need to change things so  completely and fundamentally  and why stop using hammond altogether  well as i said  jojo's bizarre adventure structure was  unlike many long running shonen at the  time  teaching him on to jonathan was great as  it gave us the audience a chance to  better understand the power system and  it was great getting to see that  progression for the main character  however when it came time for joseph to  become the protagonist  araki was then faced with an interesting  problem how does he write joseph jose to  learn hamon without  it being boring and the conclusion he  came to was that he never taught joseph  hamon he just knew it by instinct  and this was by design and interestingly  araki draws attention to this aspect of  storytelling in his book manga in theory  and practice he talks about avoiding the  temptation to go negative with your  characters and to always be progressing  in an ever rising direction  under the rule of rises and falls you  are allowed to start in a negative place  and have the movement rise from there  when it comes to that ever-rising  direction  you can't make any compromises  interestingly hammond presented araki  with a very unique problem  how can he have a series where every  single time he starts a new arc he has  to teach the protagonist how to use the  hammond power  over and over without resetting to a  point with each arc  the answer he couldn't joseph started  from a place far more advanced than  jonathan did  and really that was the last time he  could in theory up the ante on that and  the solution he came up with to remedy  this dilemma was stan's  with stans he can introduce new  characters easily and keep things  interesting without retreading old  ground because  the stan can be as wacky and different  as he needs it to be from a writing  standpoint  this while initially jarring to fans of  hamon the hamon power system  simply couldn't work long term with its  structure and thus an  elegant solution was implemented with  stance personally i really enjoyed the  implementation of stands in the story  they add an immense amount of variety to  it  and on top of that lend opportunities  for a mangaka like araki  to get as creative as possible i mean  this one was a boat and its wielder was  an orangutan  [Music]  don't do that again okay all right so  there's no avoiding the topic that  status crusaders  is really long on top of the fact that  they already fundamentally changed the  formula of jojo's bizarre adventure  last week i released a poll on my  twitter asking jojo fans in my audience  if they liked stardust crusaders the  results while not being overwhelmingly  negative did have a sizable portion  of over one in five specifically stating  that they did not like it however what  was most interesting were the comments  within the comments section  comment after comment filled that  section citing that the ark dragged on  too long and that they didn't like the  episodic standalone nature of its  telling  leading for it to feel to some people  overly boring at times  yet strangely this is the arc that  supposedly launched jojo's bizarre  adventure into the mainstream this is  the one people point to as the part when  the series really starts to get good  in other words a vocal minority disliked  it somewhat or hated the arc while  others  loved it and that dichotomy and opinion  honestly fascinated me  so much so it led me down a rabbit hole  the likes of which brought to my  attention  a newfound respect for araki as a writer  [Music]  role  [Music]  [Music]  he mentions that around the time battle  tenancy was reaching its conclusion  the series was struggling in reader  surveys  now okay i can already see a few of you  glazing over from here  so you might be wondering what are these  reader surveys well  in the case of judge's bizarre adventure  because it was released in weekly shonen  jump when araki submitted new chapters  of jojo's bizarre adventure to be  published it did so alongside numerous  other mangakas submitting their latest  chapters  these weekly shonen jump issues were  filled with tons of different  popular stories that needed to stand out  and none were more popular than akira  toriyama's dragon ball at the time  however because they were filled with so  many different stories they couldn't  rely on weekly shonen jump sales figures  and so instead  supplied readers surveys at the end of  each issue asking them what their  favorite running stories were  series that did not perform well in  these surveys consistently  ran the risk of being cut from the  publication and the more i read about  this  it became increasingly more interesting  to me that araki decided to 