he number of  random encounters they get on their way  to egypt is  quite relentless additionally that's  also predominantly why in this video it  sounds like i've only just started  talking about the story because  in this section of it there's really  very little meat to discuss beyond the  different stand users they encounter  time and time again as they get closer  to their goal of egypt  and the reason behind this seemingly  sudden and insane narrative decision  to change the format of the story turned  out to be one of the single most  interesting discoveries i made regarding  jojo's bizarre adventure and  maybe even any review i've ever done in  response to poor reader surveys during  battle tendency araki's response  was stardust crusaders an episodic  non-narratively driven  road trip across the globe to egypt at  the time as i said within the  publication weekly shonen jump dragon  ball was extremely popular among readers  and at the time dragon ball had just  finished a series of tournament arcs and  as a result editors working for shonen  jump would suggest tournaments be  written into any of the stories that  were struggling and in his book araki  speaks on this  during the height of the popularity of  the tournament manga i was able to  approach them as a reader and enjoy the  excitement but i couldn't help but  wonder from a creator's point of view  what the mangaka would do after the top  of the tournaments  at the time jojo was struggling in the  reader surveys and people even suggested  that i add a tournament format to help  me gain popularity  despite this advice to go for the safe  easily understood serialized format of a  tournament which would have also given  him plenty of opportunity to demonstrate  the stand powers in a much more easy to  control environment he instead  effectively  does the opposite not only ignoring all  the advice he was given but  turns the series on its head effectively  changing a lot of what made jojo  well jojo for 