ssionals that thought  they knew better  and instead risked everything he built  in a ridiculously competitive industry  on his own understanding of how story  works while also introducing and  establishing a brand new power system in  stands that he wasn't fully to grips  with yet  [Applause]  [Music]  that is one of the bravest things i've  ever heard a mangaka do  so often in pop culture and media do  visionaries take the safe route and copy  what's popular  i'm sure you've all noticed it when  everything game related wanted to mimic  fortnite's success in implementing a  battle royale feature  you saw when dc wanted to fast-track its  franchise into being an ensemble hero  team like the avengers and you saw it  when dragon ball success  informed the countless tournament arcs  of its time but what isn't spoken about  nearly enough are the visionaries the  true visionaries that when faced with  certain doom  grit their teeth dig their heels in and  declare that they understand  how their medium works and that they  genuinely believe in their own ideas  regardless if no one else does so and  from those truly brave writers and  illustrators you don't get stories that  try to copy something else to gain  popularity you instead get an iconic  franchise that spans  generations you don't need to be  inspired instead you're the one making  something that inspires  others and so while i haven't spoken too  much about the strange and bombastic  journey that jotaro and his friends  underwent on their way to egypt i really  don't think the important aspects of it  are the details of this particular  journey but the philosophy behind its  creation  stardust crusaders while i didn't love  its first half's format through my  research it has become a story that i  respect  more than possibly any other i've ever  read in my life  and it deserves to be known as the  iconic arc  it's become thanks for watching guys i  know i didn't cover a lot of the  meat and potatoes regarding th